missing items 	"1. Structured Outputs & JSON Mode

Detail:
Pydantic models and the Instructor library are essential for reliable tool usage. Without constrained decoding, agents often return malformed JSON, which breaks downstream processes.

2. Agent State Persistence (DB-backed)

Detail:
Long-running agents must handle crashes and restarts. In-memory state is lost on failure, and there is no implementation for persisting agent state in databases like PostgreSQL, Redis, or SQLite across sessions.

3. Context Window Management & Compression

Detail:
Agents accumulate history and hit token limits. Without strategies like summarization, sliding windows, or selective pruning, Phase 2 projects may silently degrade.

4. Agent Debugging & Trace Inspection

Detail:
When multi-agent systems produce incorrect outputs, identifying the failure point requires structured trace inspection. Currently, no debugging workflow is documented.

5. API Gateway & Rate Limit Management

Detail:
At scale, LLM rate limits can cause cascading failures. There is no implementation of token bucket strategies, exponential backoff, or multi-provider failover (e.g., OpenAI → Anthropic → Bedrock).

6. Data Privacy & PII Handling in Agents

Detail:
Agents process sensitive user data sent to third-party APIs. There is no coverage of PII detection/redaction before API calls, GDPR-compliant logging, or data residency handling.

7. Agent Unit & Integration Testing

Detail:
While LLM-as-judge evaluation exists, there is no guidance on unit testing tools, mocking LLM responses in CI, or building integration tests for full multi-agent workflows.

8. Streaming Agent Responses to UI

Detail:
Users expect real-time streaming and intermediate outputs. Although FastAPI is mentioned, streaming via SSE or WebSockets for live UIs is not covered.

9. Tool Reliability & Retry / Fallback Logic

Detail:
Tools can fail due to timeouts, 404s, or execution errors. There is no implementation for retry mechanisms, fallback strategies, or graceful degradation.

10. Computer Use / Browser Agents

Detail:
Advanced capabilities like Anthropic’s Computer Use API and screenshot-action loops are emerging. Web navigation is introduced too late (Phase 5) and should align with multi-agent systems earlier.

11. Per-run Cost Attribution & Budgeting

Detail:
While cost optimization is discussed, tracking token costs per agent run, user, or task type for billing and budgeting is not addressed.

12. Agent Design Patterns Catalogue

Detail:
There is no reference to established patterns like Plan-and-Execute, LATS, Reflexion, Self-Ask, or Skeleton-of-Thought—key for architectural discussions and design reviews.

13. Completion Status Column (Skills Sheet)

Detail:
The Skills sheet lacks a way to track completed skills. Without a status column, it acts as a reference rather than a progress tracker.

14. URL / Hyperlink Column (Resources Sheet)

Detail:
The resources sheet lists items without links, requiring manual searches. Adding a URL column would make it actionable.

15. SQL Agents & Structured Data Tools

Detail:
Text-to-SQL is a major enterprise use case. Tools like LangChain SQLDatabaseChain and Vanna.ai are not included despite being production-ready.

16. Voice & Multimodal Agent Interfaces

Detail:
Audio (Whisper + TTS) and vision-based agents (Claude Vision, GPT-4V) are growing rapidly and should be included in Phase 5 specializations.

17. Estimated API Cost per Phase

Detail:
Running agent experiments (especially multi-agent systems and fine-tuning) incurs real API costs. Learners need upfront budgeting guidance.

18. Prerequisites / Dependency Map

Detail:
There is no structured dependency mapping (e.g., “complete X before Y”). Learners lack guidance on foundational knowledge when skipping or reordering phase"
